  "prediction": "**Summary of the Report: Alignment of Federal Agencies' Physical Security Assessment Methodologies with the Interagency Security Committee's (ISC) Risk Management Standard**\n\nThe Interagency Security Committee (ISC) established a risk management standard for identifying necessary countermeasures to protect federal facilities. This report examines the alignment of four federal agencies' (Customs and Border Protection (CBP), Federal Aviation Administration (FAA), Agricultural Research Service (ARS), and United States Forest Service (Forest Service)) physical security assessment methodologies with the ISC standard.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n1. None of the four agencies' security assessment methodologies fully aligned with the ISC Standard.\n2. CBP's methodology did not consider all 33 undesirable events and assess threats and consequences.\n3. FAA's methodology did not consider all 33 undesirable events and assess all three factors of risk (threats, vulnerabilities, and consequences).\n4. ARS and the Forest Service followed an assessment methodology developed by the USDA, which did not consider all 33 undesirable events.\n5. The agencies' methodologies did not always adhere to the principles of risk management, such as considering all undesirable events and assessing threats, vulnerabilities, and consequences.\n\n**Management Challenges:**\n\n1. The agencies faced challenges in conducting timely assessments, including backlogs and insufficient resources.\n2. The agencies lacked the means to collect, store, and analyze risk assessment information to monitor the status of a facility's security.\n3. The agencies' methods of collecting and storing security information had limitations that affected agency and facility officials' oversight of the physical security of their facilities.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n1. The Commissioner of U.S. Customs and Border Protection should update the Security Policy and Procedures Handbook to align with the ISC Standard.\n2. The Secretary of Transportation should direct the FAA Administrator to develop a plan to implement an improved methodology and update FAA's policy to require the use of a methodology that fully aligns with the ISC Standard.\n3. The Secretary of Agriculture should direct the Administrator of the Agricultural Research Service and the Chief of the Forest Service to implement and monitor a long-term assessment schedule with key milestones to ensure that higher-level facilities are reassessed at least once every 3 years.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThe four federal agencies examined in this report have not fully aligned their physical security assessment methodologies with the ISC Standard. To address these issues, the agencies should update their policies and procedures to require methodologies that consider all 33 undesirable events and assess threats, vulnerabilities, and consequences. Additionally, the agencies should develop plans to address backlogs and improve",
